===========================================
## STATEFUL vs STATELESS ARCHITECTURE ?
===========================================

Pichle chapter (Scaling) me humne dekha ki Horizontal Scaling ke baad
application ke multiple instances run karte hain.

Agar user ki session information kisi ek server ki local memory (RAM)
me store ho, to Load Balancer user ki next request kisi doosre server
par bhej sakta hai.

Us server ke paas previous session information available nahi hogi,
jisse session-related problems create ho sakti hain.

Is problem ko properly samajhne ke liye pehle hume
Stateful aur Stateless Architecture samajhna hoga.

-------------------
## WHAT IS STATE ?
-------------------
State simply means information that a system remembers and uses while processing future requests.


-----------------------
## WHAT IS STATELESS ?
-----------------------

Stateless application previous requests ki information (state) remember nahi karti.

Har request independently process hoti hai, aur us request ko process karne ke liye required saari information usi request ke saath aati hai.

-----------------------
## WHAT IS STATEFUL ?
-----------------------

A Stateful application previous requests ki information (state) ko remember karti hai aur future requests ko process karte time usi stored information ka use karti hai.


--------------------------------------
## WHY DO WE NEED TO MAINTAIN STATE?
--------------------------------------

HTTP protocol by default Stateless hota hai. Matlab server ko previous requests yaad nahi rehti.

Lekin real-world applications me user ko har request par dobara login karwana practical nahi hai.

Isliye applications ko kisi mechanism ki zarurat hoti hai jo multiple HTTP / HTTPS requests ke beech user-related information maintain kar sake.

Traditional Java web applications is purpose ke liye HttpSession use karti hain.


---------------------------
## WHAT IS HTTP SESSION ?
---------------------------

HTTP / HTTPS (HTTP + TLS/SSL) protocol by default stateless hota hai.

Matlab server ko by default ye yaad nahi rehta ki
current request kis user ne bheji hai aur kya us user ne
isse pehle koi request bheji thi.

Lekin real-world applications me hume user ki kuch information
multiple requests ke beech maintain karni padti hai.

HttpSession ek server-side object (simple language me, ek server-side container) hai
jo Servlet Container (e.g., Tomcat) provide karta hai.
Ye user/client se related temporary information ko
multiple HTTP requests ke beech maintain karta hai.


## LOGIN-BASED APPLICATIONS ME :
----------------------------------

Application (e.g. Spring Security) pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. verify karti hai ki username/password ya other credentials valid hain).

Authentication successful hone ke baad application
Servlet Container (e.g., Tomcat) se HttpSession create karne ke liye request karti hai.

Servlet Container HttpSession create karta hai,
jisme application required user-related temporary information
store karti hai.

Ye information subsequent HTTP/HTTPS requests ko
process karte waqt use ki ja sakti hai.


Authentication typically involves verifying the user's credentials
against a Database, LDAP server, OAuth Provider, or another
Identity Provider, depending on the application's authentication mechanism.


Baad me aane wali HTTP/HTTPS requests me isi HttpSession ki help se
user ko identify kiya ja sakta hai (Authentication Context),
aur uske Roles / Permissions ke basis par Authorization
perform ki ja sakti hai.


## EXAMPLES OF INFORMATION STORED IN HTTP SESSION :
-----------------------------------------------------

├── Logged-in User ID
├── Username
├── User Roles (ADMIN / USER)
├── Authentication Status
├── CSRF Token
├── Temporary OTP Verification State
├── Multi-step Form Data
├── User Preferences (Language, Theme)
└── Other temporary user-specific information



## NOTE:
----------

1: Technically Shopping Cart jaise business data ko HttpSession
   me store kiya ja sakta hai, lekin modern distributed applications
   generally aisa avoid karti hain.

   Business data (Cart, Wishlist, Orders, etc.) usually Database
   me store hota hai, jabki HttpSession sirf temporary user context
   (e.g. Logged-in User, Roles, Authentication Status) maintain
   karne ke liye use hota hai.

   Isliye HttpSession expire hone, server restart/crash hone ke baad bhi important business data available rehta hai.


2: HttpSession khud koi storage technology (jaise Redis, Database, ya File System) nahi hai.
   Ye sirf ek server side Session Object hai.
   By default, Tomcat jaise Servlet Containers is Session Object ko server ki local memory (RAM) me store karte hain.


3. HttpSession sirf authenticated users ke liye limited nahi hai.
   Anonymous (non-logged-in) users ke liye bhi HttpSession
   use kiya ja sakta hai.

   Example:

    Anonymous User
          ↓
    Add Product to Cart
          ↓
    HttpSession
          ↓
    Temporary Cart



