## PROBLEMS WITH HTTP SESSION IN DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS
---------------------------------------------------------

HttpSession user ki session-related information ko multiple HTTP requests ke beech maintain
karne ka kaam karti hai.

Single Server architecture me ye approach kaafi simple
aur effective hoti hai.

Example:

            User
              |
              v
      Spring Boot + Tomcat
              |
      HttpSession (RAM)

Har request isi server par aati hai.

Isliye server easily corresponding HttpSession
retrieve karke user ko identify kar leta hai.

Is approach ki kuch limitations hain.


## PROBLEM 1: HORIZONTAL SCALING
----------------------------------

                     User
                       |
                       |
                  Load Balancer
                 /      |      \
                /       |       \
               v        v        v

          Server-1  Server-2  Server-3
         (Session)   (No)       (No)


Suppose user login karta hai.

Load Balancer login request ko

    Server-1

par bhejta hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad Server-1
Servlet Container (Tomcat) ke through ek HttpSession
create karta hai, usse ek unique Session ID assign
karta hai aur us Session ID ko HttpSession ke saath
internally map (bind) kar deta hai.

Uske baad response ke saath browser ko ek cookie
bheji jaati hai jisme sirf Session ID hoti hai.

Example:

|--------------------------------------------
|                                            |
| Session ID : ABC123XYZ                     |
|                                            |
|        |                                   |
|        v                                   |
|                                            |
|  HttpSession Object                        |
|                                            |
|    User = Anurag                           |
|    Role = USER                             |
|    Last Access Time                        |
|    Creation Time                           |
|                                            |
 --------------------------------------------

Ab browser ke paas

    JSESSIONID = ABC123XYZ

cookie aa gayi.

Ab user jab next HTTP request bhejta hai,
to browser automatically is request ke saath
JSESSIONID cookie bhi bhej deta hai.

Is baar Load Balancer request ko

    Server-2

par forward kar deta hai.

Server-2 browser se

    JSESSIONID = ABC123XYZ

receive karega.

Lekin Server-2 ki local memory me

    ABC123XYZ

wali HttpSession exist hi nahi karti,

kyunki wo sirf Server-1 ki local memory (RAM)
me stored thi.

## RESULT:
----------

Server-2 corresponding HttpSession retrieve
nahi kar paata.

Isliye application user ko identify nahi kar paata
aur request ko unauthenticated treat kar sakti hai.

As a result (depending on the application implementation):

    - User ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.
    - User ko "Session Expired" message dikhaya ja sakta hai.
    - Web applications me Login Page par redirect kiya ja sakta hai.
    - REST APIs me 401 Unauthorized response return kiya ja sakta hai.



## PROBLEM 2: SERVER RESTART / SERVER CRASH
---------------------------------------------

By default HttpSession server ki local memory (RAM)
me store hoti hai.

Agar jis server par HttpSession stored hai,
wo restart ya crash ho jaye,

to us server ki RAM clear ho jaati hai.

Result:

- Saari active HttpSessions destroy ho jaati hain.
- Users ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.


## PROBLEM 3: HIGH RAM CONSUMPTION
--------------------------------------

HttpSession by default server ki RAM me store hoti hai.

Har logged-in user ke liye ek alag HttpSession Object
maintain kiya jata hai.

Agar application me lakhs of active users ho,

to server ko lakhs of HttpSession Objects
memory me maintain karne pad sakte hain.

Result:

RAM usage increase hoti hai.
Frequent Garbage Collection ho sakti hai.
Memory pressure increase hota hai.
OutOfMemoryError ka risk badh sakta hai.


--------------------------------------------
## HOW CAN WE OVERCOME THESE LIMITATIONS ?
--------------------------------------------

Traditional HttpSession ki in limitations ko overcome
karne ke liye multiple approaches use ki jaati hain.

Common approaches:


## APPROACH 1: STICKY SESSIONS
------------------------------------

               User
                 |
          Load Balancer
                 |
        -----------------
        |               |
        v               v

    Server-1        Server-2


Sticky Session use karne par Load Balancer jis server par
user ki initial login request forward karta hai, HttpSession usi server ki
local memory (RAM) me create aur store hoti hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad Load Balancer user aur backend server ke
beech ek mapping maintain kar leta hai.

Uske baad Load Balancer us user ki subsequent requests bhi
usi server par forward karta rehta hai, taaki har request ke liye
corresponding HttpSession available rahe.

Yaani ek user ka session lifecycle generally ek hi server se
associated rehta hai.

Isi wajah se Sticky Sessions Horizontal Scaling ki session-related
problem ko kaafi had tak solve kar deti hain.

Lekin is approach ki bhi kuch important limitations hain.


1. SERVER FAILURE
-------------------

Suppose User-1 ki HttpSession Server-1 ki local memory (RAM)
me stored hai.

Agar Server-1 crash ya restart ho jaye,

to uski RAM me stored HttpSession bhi destroy ho jaati hai.

Result:
- User ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.
- Ya application user ko "Session Expired" dikha sakti hai.


2. LOAD IMBALANCE WITH STICKY SESSIONS
---------------------------------------

Suppose initially application do servers par deployed thi.

                Users
                  |
                  v
           Load Balancer
             /       \
            /         \
           v           v

      Server-1     Server-2


Users gradually login karte gaye.

Sticky Sessions ki wajah se kuch users ki sessions Server-1
se associate ho gayi aur baaki users ki sessions Server-2
se associate ho gayi.

Baad me traffic aur increase hua.

Company ne Horizontal Scaling karke do naye servers add kar diye.


                  Load Balancer
              /      |      |      \
             /       |      |       \
            v        v      v        v

      Server-1  Server-2  Server-3  Server-4


Lekin pehle se logged-in users ki HttpSession abhi bhi active hai (i.e. users ne logout nahi kiya hai aur session expire bhi nahi hui hai).

Sticky Sessions ki wajah se un users ki future requests ab bhi Server-1 aur Server-2 par hi route ki jaati hain.

Sirf naye users hi  naye servers i.e Server-3 aur Server-4 par distribute honge.


Example:

Server-1 : 600 Existing Users
Server-2 : 550 Existing Users
Server-3 :  40 New Users
Server-4 :  35 New Users

Result:

Server-1 aur Server-2 par comparatively zyada load reh sakta hai,

jabki Server-3 aur Server-4 underutilized reh sakte hain.

Is wajah se Load Balancer available servers ke beech requests ko
efficiently distribute nahi kar paata, aur overall load balancing ki
efficiency reduce ho sakti hai.



APPROACH 2 : SESSION REPLICATION
------------------------------------

Sticky Sessions Horizontal Scaling ki session-related problems ko partially solve karti hain.

Lekin is approach ki kuch important limitations ab bhi rehti hain:

1: Agar jis server ki local memory me HttpSession stored hai wo crash ya restart ho jaye, to us server ki HttpSession destroy ho jaati hai.
2: Sticky Sessions ki wajah se Load Balancer har user ki requests ko repeatedly usi server par bhejta hai, jisse available servers ke beech requests efficiently distribute nahi ho paati aur load imbalance create ho sakta hai.

In limitations ko overcome karne ke liye Session Replication approach use ki ja sakti hai.

Session Replication me user ki HttpSession sirf ek server ki local memory me store nahi hoti, balki cluster ke multiple servers ke saath replicate (synchronize) bhi ki jaati hai.

                   
                               Load Balancer
                                     |
        -------------------------------------------------------------------
        |                      |                     |                     |
        |                      |                     |                     |
        |                      |                     |                     |
        |                      |                     |                     |
      Server-1  <--------->  Server-2 <--------->  Server-3  <--------->  Server-4
       Session               Session     Session               Session

           ▲                    ▲                     ▲                     ▲
        Replication         Replication            Replication         Replication



## HOW DOES IT WORK?
----------------------

Suppose user login karta hai.

Load Balancer login request ko

Server-1

par forward karta hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad Server-1 (via servlet container like tomcat) HttpSession create karta hai Server 1 ki RAM mai.

Lekin is baar HttpSession sirf Server-1 ki local memory (RAM) me store nahi hoti.

Server-1 us HttpSession ko background me Server-2 (aur cluster ke baaki servers) ke saath bhi replicate (synchronize) kar deta hai.

Yaani same HttpSession multiple servers ki memory me available hoti hai.

Ab agar user ki next request

Server-2

par aa jaye,

to Server-2 ke paas bhi usi Session ID ke corresponding HttpSession available hogi.

Isliye Server-2 user ko successfully identify kar sakta hai aur user ko dobara login karne ki zarurat nahi padti.

Agar baad me Server-1 crash ya restart bhi ho jaye,

to HttpSession ki replicated copy Server-2 par available rehti hai.

Isliye user apna existing session continue kar sakta hai aur request normally process ho sakti hai.


## LIMITATIONS
----------------

1. MEMORY OVERHEAD
-------------------

Har server ko apne HTTP Sessions ke alawa dusre servers ki sessions bhi store karni padti hain. i.e own session + replicated sessions.


2. NETWORK OVERHEAD & POOR SCALABILITY
---------------------------------------

Session Replication me har HttpSession ko cluster ke multiple
servers ke saath synchronize (replicate) karna padta hai.

Ye replication sirf Session create hone par hi nahi,
balki Session update hone par bhi hoti hai.

## NOTE:

Session Update ka matlab hai HttpSession ke andar stored
session data (attributes) ya session metadata me koi bhi change hona.

Examples:

- User ki Last Access Time update hona.
- User ki Language Preference change hona.
- User ke Roles / Permissions update hona.

## EXAMPLE:

Suppose HttpSession Timeout

    30 Minutes

hai.

User ne

    10:00 AM

par login kiya.

Agar user

    10:20 AM

par koi request bhejta hai,

to Tomcat Session ki Last Access Time update kar deta hai.

Is update ko bhi cluster ke baaki servers ke saath
replicate karna padta hai.

Yaani sirf HttpSession creation hi nahi,
HttpSession updates bhi replication trigger karti hain.

Jaise-jaise cluster ka size badhta hai,
replication traffic bhi continuously increase hota jaata hai.

Suppose:

    50 Servers

aur

    100000 Active Sessions

Har HttpSession ko multiple servers ke saath replicate
karna practical nahi hota.

## RESULT:

- Increased Network Traffic
- Higher Bandwidth Consumption
- Reduced Scalability


3. REPLICATION LAG
-------------------

Suppose User-1 login karta hai.

Load Balancer login request ko

    Server-1

par forward karta hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad Server-1 HttpSession create karta hai.

Uske baad Server-1 us HttpSession ko cluster ke baaki servers ke saath replicate (synchronize) karna start karta hai.

Lekin Session Replication instant nahi hoti.

Replication ke liye network communication aur processing time lagta hai.

Isi beech agar User-1 ki next request aa jaye aur Load Balancer us request ko

    Server-2

par forward kar de,

to ho sakta hai us samay tak HttpSession abhi Server-2 par replicate na hui ho.


## RESULT:

Server-2 corresponding HttpSession retrieve nahi kar paayega.

Application user ko unauthenticated treat kar sakti hai.

As a result (depending on the application implementation):

    - User ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.
    - "Session Expired" message dikhaya ja sakta hai.
    - Web application me Login Page par redirect kiya ja sakta hai.
    - REST API me 401 Unauthorized response return ho sakta hai.

Is temporary delay ko Replication Lag kehte hain.



